<h1>CyberBuddy Threat Detection - End-to-End Audit Report</h1>
<h2>PHASE 1 — PROJECT DISCOVERY</h2>
<h3>Project Overview</h3>
<p>The "CyberBuddy Threat Detection" project is an application designed to analyze emails and URLs to detect phishing and other cyber threats using Machine Learning.</p>
<h3>Dependency Map &amp; Architecture</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Frontend Framework:</strong> Vanilla HTML/CSS/JS with some pages (Login/Register) utilizing Tailwind CSS via CDN, leading to an inconsistent UI architecture.</li>
<li><strong>Backend Framework:</strong> Flask (Python).</li>
<li><strong>Database:</strong> Flat-file CSV (<code>users.csv</code>) acting as a makeshift database for user credentials.</li>
<li><strong>Machine Learning:</strong> Scikit-Learn (<code>LogisticRegression</code>) relying on custom NLP preprocessing via <code>nltk</code> and feature extraction.</li>
<li><strong>Authentication:</strong> Custom, highly insecure implementation using direct CSV lookups.</li>
<li><strong>APIs:</strong> The backend exposes <code>/scan</code> for AJAX requests and <code>/analyze</code> for form submissions.</li>
<li><strong>External Services:</strong> None identified.</li>
<li><strong>Build/Deployment:</strong> No build system, Dockerfile, or deployment configurations exist.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Request Flow</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>User Flow:</strong> User accesses <code>/</code>, submits credentials to <code>/login</code>. Upon successful login, they are redirected to <code>/dashboard</code>.</li>
<li><strong>Data Flow:</strong> The user pastes an email/URL in the dashboard. The form POSTs to <code>/analyze</code> (or via AJAX to <code>/scan</code>).</li>
<li><strong>ML Prediction Flow:</strong> The backend receives the string -&gt; <code>clean_text()</code> removes URLs, symbols, stopwords -&gt; <code>extract_features()</code> counts keywords/lengths -&gt; <code>pandas.DataFrame</code> is created -&gt; <code>model.predict()</code> evaluates the risk -&gt; result is rendered to the user.</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>PHASE 2 — RUNNABILITY AUDIT</h2>
<p><strong>Result: FAIL ❌</strong>
The application cannot successfully run and evaluate requests on a clean machine.</p>
<h3>Startup Failures &amp; Missing Components</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Missing NLTK Resources:</strong></li>
<li><em>Root Cause:</em> The <code>preprocessing.py</code> script relies on <code>punkt_tab</code> and <code>stopwords</code> from NLTK, but the app lacks an initialization step to download them (<code>nltk.download('punkt')</code>).</li>
<li><em>Impact:</em> Any attempt to analyze text (via <code>/analyze</code> or <code>/scan</code>) crashes the Flask server with a <code>LookupError</code>.</li>
<li><em>Fix:</em> Add a setup script or initialize NLTK downloads in <code>app.py</code> before loading the model.</li>
<li><em>Severity:</em> <strong>CRITICAL</strong></li>
<li><strong>Missing Dependencies:</strong></li>
<li><em>Root Cause:</em> <code>nltk</code> is heavily used but entirely missing from <code>requirements.txt</code>.</li>
<li><em>Impact:</em> <code>pip install -r requirements.txt</code> will yield a broken environment.</li>
<li><em>Severity:</em> <strong>HIGH</strong></li>
<li><strong>Missing Datasets:</strong></li>
<li><em>Root Cause:</em> The ML training scripts (<code>train_baseline.py</code>, <code>train_enhanced.py</code>) rely on <code>datasets/email_dataset.csv</code>, but the <code>datasets</code> directory does not exist in the repository.</li>
<li><em>Impact:</em> The ML model cannot be reproduced or retrained.</li>
<li><em>Severity:</em> <strong>HIGH</strong></li>
<li><strong>Incorrect File Paths:</strong></li>
<li><em>Root Cause:</em> <code>app.py</code> (Lines 58, 89, 99) hardcodes the path <code>backend/users.csv</code>. If the Flask app is run from inside the <code>backend</code> folder, it will crash with a <code>FileNotFoundError</code>.</li>
<li><em>Impact:</em> Fragile execution environment.</li>
<li><em>Severity:</em> <strong>MEDIUM</strong></li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>PHASE 3 — CODE REVIEW</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="text-align: left;">File</th>
<th style="text-align: left;">Line</th>
<th style="text-align: left;">Severity</th>
<th style="text-align: left;">Description</th>
<th style="text-align: left;">Recommended Fix</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;"><code>app.py</code></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">30</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">Low</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">Unused <code>USERS_FILE</code> variable defined using <code>BASE_DIR</code>, but hardcoded <code>"backend/users.csv"</code> is used instead.</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">Use <code>USERS_FILE</code> consistently for all file I/O operations.</td>
</tr>
