<hr />
<h2>PHASE 4 — SECURITY AUDIT</h2>
<p><strong>Result: SEVERELY COMPROMISED 🛑</strong></p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="text-align: left;">Vulnerability</th>
<th style="text-align: left;">Severity</th>
<th style="text-align: left;">Description</th>
<th style="text-align: left;">Exploit Scenario</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>No Session Management / Broken Access Control</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>CRITICAL</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;"><code>/dashboard</code> has no authentication checks. The <code>/login</code> route merely redirects to <code>/dashboard</code> upon success but sets no cookies or session state.</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">An attacker can entirely bypass the login screen by navigating directly to <code>http://localhost:5000/dashboard</code>.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>Plaintext Password Storage</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>CRITICAL</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">Passwords are saved in <code>users.csv</code> without hashing or salting.</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">If the CSV is exposed or leaked, all user passwords are immediately compromised.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>Insecure Password Reset</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>CRITICAL</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">The <code>/forgot-password</code> route allows a password reset by simply providing a known username and email without any verification link, token, or old password check.</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">An attacker can change any user's password if they know their email and username.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>Insecure Deserialization</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>HIGH</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">The ML model is loaded using Python's <code>pickle.load()</code>.</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">If an attacker can replace <code>threat_model.pkl</code>, loading it will execute arbitrary remote code (RCE) on the server.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>Race Conditions</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>MEDIUM</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;"><code>users.csv</code> is opened, read, and rewritten synchronously.</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">Two users registering at the exact same millisecond will corrupt or wipe the database.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>PHASE 5 — MACHINE LEARNING REVIEW</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Reproducibility:</strong> <strong>FAILED.</strong> The <code>datasets</code> folder is missing, making it impossible to audit the data distribution or retrain the model.</li>
<li><strong>Feature Engineering:</strong> Basic and flawed. It relies heavily on arbitrary keyword counts. Furthermore, the <code>has_url</code> feature is completely neutralized by a data leakage/pipeline order bug.</li>
<li><strong>Model Loading:</strong> The model is an <code>sklearn</code> Logistic Regression model. A warning (<code>InconsistentVersionWarning</code>) indicates it was trained on scikit-learn 1.8.0 but might be run on older versions, which can lead to unpredictable behavior.</li>
<li><strong>Scalability:</strong> Wrapping single dictionaries into <code>pd.DataFrame</code> for single-item inference in a Flask route is inefficient and will bottleneck under heavy load.</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>PHASE 6 — FRONTEND REVIEW</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Broken HTML:</strong> <code>frontend/templates/index.html</code> contains two complete <code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;&lt;html&gt;...&lt;/html&gt;</code> blocks concatenated together in the same file.</li>
<li><strong>Inconsistent UI Frameworks:</strong> <code>login.html</code> and <code>register.html</code> use Tailwind CSS via a CDN. <code>index.html</code> relies on a local <code>auth.css</code>. This leads to jarring visual shifts between pages.</li>
<li><strong>Missing Features:</strong> There is no "Logout" button on the dashboard.</li>
<li><strong>Form Validation:</strong> <code>register.html</code> lacks client-side password matching validation. The backend handles it, but client-side feedback is missing.</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>PHASE 7 — TESTING</h2>
<p><strong>Testing Summary</strong>
* <strong>Passed Tests:</strong> 0
* <strong>Failed Tests:</strong> 2 (<code>test_features.py</code> and <code>test_preprocessing.py</code> fail immediately due to missing <code>nltk</code> packages).
* <strong>Untested Areas:</strong> 100% of the core application logic, API routes, and ML predictions lack automated unit, integration, or functional tests.</p>
<hr />
<h2>PHASE 8 — PERFORMANCE REVIEW</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Data I/O:</strong> Reading and writing to <code>users.csv</code> line-by-line during every login/register request involves heavy disk I/O and will critically bottleneck the application.</li>
<li><strong>Inference Overhead:</strong> Instantiating Pandas DataFrames per-request in the <code>/analyze</code> and <code>/scan</code> routes adds unnecessary overhead. The model should ideally accept numpy arrays or optimized data structures.</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>PHASE 9 — DOCUMENTATION REVIEW</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>README:</strong> Exists, but is only 3 lines long.</li>
<li><strong>Missing Components:</strong></li>
<li>No <code>pip install -r requirements.txt</code> instructions.</li>
<li>No Python version requirements.</li>
<li>No instructions on how to download NLTK datasets.</li>
<li>No instructions on how to run the Flask server.</li>
<li>No deployment guidelines.</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>PHASE 10 — FINAL REPORT</h2>
<h3>Executive Summary</h3>
<p>The CyberBuddy Threat Detection application is currently in an unrunnable, insecure, and untested prototype state. While the concept is clear, the implementation suffers from critical security flaws, major architecture missteps, and missing files that prevent execution. The repository is far from production-ready.</p>
